    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        The Narcissus Hypothesis: Descending to the Rung of Illusion
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Cadei%2C+R">Riccardo Cadei</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Intern%C3%B2%2C+C">Christian Internò</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2509.17999v4-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Modern <span class="search-hit mathjax">foundational</span>&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2509.17999v4-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2509.17999v4-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2509.17999v4-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Modern <span class="search-hit mathjax">foundational</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> increasingly reflect not just world knowledge, but patterns of human preference embedded in their <span class="search-hit mathjax">training</span> data. We hypothesize that recursive alignment-via human feedback and model-generated corpora-induces a social desirability bias, nudging models to favor agreeable or flattering responses over objective <span class="search-hit mathjax">reasoning</span>. We refer to it as the Narcissus Hypothesis and test it across 31 models using standardized personality assessments and a novel Social Desirability Bias score. Results reveal a significant drift toward socially conforming traits, with profound implications for corpus integrity and the reliability of downstream inferences. We then offer a novel epistemological interpretation, tracing how recursive bias may collapse higher-order <span class="search-hit mathjax">reasoning</span> down Pearl&#39;s Ladder of Causality, culminating in what we refer to as the Rung of Illusion.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2509.17999v4-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2509.17999v4-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 21 October, 2025; <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">v1</span> submitted 22 September, 2025;
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> September 2025.
      
    </p>
    
